# Backpack LLM

Shared C++ chat and benchmark application for GGUF and supported ONNX models.
All commands for the active goal run on webgfx-104 (RTX 5080) only.

Generation honors the model's end-of-sequence, end-of-turn, and end-of-message
markers. Benchmarks continue to produce the exact requested token count.

Ordinary chat output omits internal capture summaries. Set `BP_EXEC_STATS=1`
when diagnosing capture, replay parameters, or graph execution; this diagnostic
mode includes internal statistics and is not a clean answer or timing run.

Build from the repository root:

```powershell
python -B build.py runtime
```

The executable is `gitignore/runtime/build/backpack_llm.exe`. Shader embedding
is automatic; build outputs and generated headers stay under `gitignore/`.

## Qwen3.8-27B on this device

The tested artifact is Unsloth `Qwen3.8-27B-UD-IQ3_S.gguf`, revision
`4ca720788d1e01f1bff70c033e0d0028fd02e502`. Use the bounded context below:

```powershell
gitignore/runtime/build/backpack_llm.exe --model gitignore/models/Qwen3.8-27B-GGUF/Qwen3.8-27B-UD-IQ3_S.gguf --backend d3d12 --max-seq-len 1024 --max-tokens 128 --chat "Explain why the sky is blue."
```

`--chat` applies the Qwen non-thinking template with the common system message.
Windows arguments are converted from UTF-16 to UTF-8, so Chinese prompts also
work. Generation defaults to deterministic greedy decoding. The app rejects
requests whose prompt plus generation limit exceeds the configured context.

The runtime keeps the mixed quantized projections and input embedding table
compressed on the GPU and loads the model's separate output head. The appended
MTP block is excluded from the 64-layer target decoder; speculative MTP is not
enabled by this command.

Batched prefill with bounded native weight staging is selected automatically
for this artifact on RTX 5080. The accepted `859a23d` application measurement
records 142.34 prefill tokens/s and 11.98 decode tokens/s for 512 input tokens,
128 generated tokens and context 640. Prefill includes the first prediction;
the remaining 127 decode calls are timed separately. Exact packed IQ4_XS lookup
improves decode by 3.75% over `e5e25be`, with neutral prefill. Shared chat,
sampling and benchmark flows are validated, but the large gap to llama.cpp
remains open. See `docs/webgfx-104-enablement.md` for revision-linked evidence.

## Qwen3.8-27B ONNX on webgfx-104

The shared application runs the text decoder from
`amd/Qwen3.8-27B-fp16-ve-fp16-int4-k_quant-gs128-text-onnx`, pinned to revision
`3d00750aaa18e3e09e0b271a82e7762a4586718f`. Its validated configuration is available in the main workspace build.
From the workspace root:

```powershell
gitignore/runtime/build/backpack_llm.exe --model gitignore/models/Qwen3.8-27B-ONNX --backend d3d12 --max-seq-len 640 --prefill-chunk 32 --max-tokens 128 --chat "Explain why the sky is blue in one sentence."
```

`--prefill-chunk` bounds each generic ONNX prompt batch to control temporary
memory. The runtime processes the chunks in order, retaining recurrent state
and attention caches. Whole-prompt capture is disabled for multi-chunk prompts;
decode capture remains enabled. Reference comparisons must use the same batch
schedule because FP16 recurrent state is rounded at each batch boundary.

The oversized FP16 embedding table is mapped on the CPU; selected token rows
are copied to the GPU. Media placeholders are rejected. This is text-only
enablement on the RTX 5080 using D3D12, without subgroup-matrix operations.

At context capacity 640, the 512-input/128-output diagnostic with 32-token
batches matches all 128 tokens from the original native ORT WebGPU export.
Peak logical GPU allocation was 15.15 GiB; live allocation after decode was
13.40 GiB. These counters exclude free buffer pools and driver staging, and do
not establish full physical residency. Three complete short sky responses have
stable live allocations and identical output. The sky response matches the
higher-accuracy ORT CPU diagnostic; the published accuracy-level-4 reference
chooses different wording. See `gitignore/logs/qwen38-onnx/` for exact evidence.
A matched 512-input/128-output baseline is recorded at accepted revision
`ebbf8db`: 20.19 prefill tokens/s and 0.816 decode tokens/s. Its decode CV is
8.07%, above the unchanged 5% acceptance limit; all samples remain recorded.
Newer memory/default and generic-session-ownership candidates remain unselected.
These historical rates do not claim current timing from a correctness-only run.
See `docs/webgfx-104-enablement.md` for the selected runtime and pending work.

## Benchmarks and validation

The `fix/webgfx104-benchmark-protocol` experiment provides exact workload
measurements in `gitignore/runtime/build/backpack_llm.exe`.
For example, from the workspace root:

```powershell
gitignore/runtime/build/backpack_llm.exe --model D:/workspace/project/agents/ai-models/Qwen3.5-2B/onnx-webgpu --backend d3d12 --max-seq-len 640 --benchmark --bench-prompt-len 512 --bench-gen-tokens 128 --bench-warmup 1 --bench-repetitions 5 --bench-json gitignore/logs/qwen2b-exact-benchmark.json
```

The default workload is 512 space-separated `A` tokens. Supply `--prompt` or
`--bench-prompt-file` to use different raw text; `--bench-prompt-len` checks the
actual count. Benchmark text does not acquire an implicit chat/BOS prefix.
The C++ `BenchmarkTokens` API also accepts an exact token vector.

Each warmup runs a complete workload, then resets before measurement. A
128-token result contains the first prediction from prefill and exactly 127
decode calls. No unreported warmup tokens advance the measured context.
Prefill timing includes the first prediction; native ORT comparisons must add
its separate first-token sampling time to prompt processing. JSON retains the
actual prompt/output IDs, counts, final position and per-run timings. Changed
continuations or WebGPU execution errors fail the benchmark.

Protocol `llm-fixed-text-reuse-first-token-v2` identifies these measurements.
A bounded generic ONNX batch adds a suffix such as `-chunk32`; compare only
matching batch schedules. Earlier synthetic-prompt and hidden-decode-warmup
results are not comparable with this protocol. The benchmark uses the same
selected prefill path as the application, including `--fast-prefill` where
explicitly enabled.

```powershell
gitignore/runtime/build/backpack_llm.exe --model gitignore/models/Qwen3.8-27B-GGUF/Qwen3.8-27B-UD-IQ3_S.gguf --backend d3d12 --max-seq-len 1024 --fast-prefill --benchmark --bench-prompt-len 512 --bench-gen-tokens 128 --save-baseline gitignore/logs/qwen38-benchmark.json

python -B benchmarks/validate_qwen_gguf.py --model gitignore/models/Qwen3.8-27B-GGUF/Qwen3.8-27B-UD-IQ3_S.gguf --reference gitignore/evolution/backups/llamacpp/b11256/vulkan/llama-completion.exe --include-batched
```

The benchmark reports prefill and decode separately. Run clean measurements
without `--profile`; profiling changes timing. The conformance runner stores
commands, binary hashes, both output streams, and exact-output verdicts under
`gitignore/logs/`. It executes the two runtimes sequentially.

## Other models and formats

The same executable accepts GGUF files and supported ONNX package directories:

```powershell
gitignore/runtime/build/backpack_llm.exe --model D:/workspace/project/agents/ai-models/Qwen3.5-2B-GGUF/Qwen3.5-2B-Q4_K_M.gguf --backend d3d12 --chat "Answer with only the number. What is 2 + 2?" --max-tokens 8
```

The working-tree executable now runs the current Gemma 4 E2B and
Qwen3.5-2B/4B ONNX packages, using the compatibility fixes validated at `111d7be`.
The broader performance audit and milestone publication remain pending.

```powershell
gitignore/runtime/build/backpack_llm.exe --model D:/workspace/project/agents/ai-models/gemma-4-E2B-it/onnx-webgpu --backend d3d12 --max-seq-len 1024 --max-tokens 16 --chat "What is the capital of France?"

gitignore/runtime/build/backpack_llm.exe --model D:/workspace/project/agents/ai-models/Qwen3.5-2B/onnx-webgpu --backend d3d12 --max-seq-len 1024 --max-tokens 32 --chat "Explain why the sky is blue in one sentence."
```

Gemma matches native ORT CPU/WebGPU on four checked workloads, including a
520-token recall prompt. Both Qwen models pass exact-answer checks; numerical
comparisons and capture behavior are documented in `docs/webgfx-104-enablement.md`.
Qwen3.8 ONNX validation is documented above. Artifact-specific evidence and
remaining performance gates are retained in `gitignore/logs/qwen38-onnx/`.

On RTX 5080/D3D12, Gemma 4 E2B ONNX prompts longer than 16 tokens now use
batched prefill by default. The validated 512/128 workload improves prefill
about 8.1 times while preserving exact output and decode throughput. Set
`BP_GEMMA_SERIAL_PREFILL=1` to select the previous serial path. GGUF uses its
existing prefill route. Streaming session reset drains outstanding decode
readbacks before accepting the next prompt.

Useful options: `--prompt` for already formatted text, `--format gguf|onnx`,
`--max-tokens`, `--max-seq-len`, `--prefill-chunk`, `--backend`, `--benchmark`, `--bench-prompt-len`,
`--bench-gen-tokens`, and `--save-baseline <path>`. The C++ API is `bp::Device`
and `bp::LmSession` in `runtime/backpack.h` and `runtime/lm_session.h`.
